    "text": "Wait, if population = 120,000.\nSpots per giraffe = ~150 (some sources say 100-200).\n120,000 * 150 = 18,000,000.\nIf population = 117,000.\nSpots = 150.\nTotal = 17,550,000.\n\nLet's refine the spots per giraffe.\nA giraffe's spots can range from large patches to smaller ones. Wait, some sources say they have a few hundred, maybe 200. Let's assume 200.\n117,000 * 200 = 23.4 million.\nIf it's 23.4 million, then it naturally exceeds the threshold.\nIf it's 17.5 million, it doesn't.\n\nLet's do a more rigorous estimate.\nGiraffe population:\n- Masai: ~35,000\n- Southern: ~50,000\n- Northern: ~5,000\n- Reticulated: ~15,000\n- Total: ~105,000 to 117,000. Let's use 110,000.\n\nSpots per giraffe by species:\n- Masai: irregular, star-like. Often 100-200.\n- Southern: rounded, large. Often 50-100.\n- Northern: similar to Southern, ~50-100.\n- Reticulated: very defined, polygonal, covering whole body. Usually 200-300.\n\nLet's average it out:\n- Masai (35k) * 150 = 5,250,000\n- Southern (50k) * 75 = 3,750,000\n- Northern (5k) * 75 = 375,000\n- Reticulated (15k) * 250 = 3,750,000\nTotal spots = 5,250,000 + 3,750,000 + 375,000 + 3,750,000 = 12,725,000.\n\nWait, a 2010 study or general giraffe facts: \"A giraffe has about 200 spots\".\nIf average spots = 100, total = 11,000,000.\nIf average spots = 200, total = 22,000,000.\nLet's use 150 as the global average.\n110,000 giraffes * 150 = 16,500,000.\n\nWhat if we consider legs and faces? A giraffe's spots cover most of the body, but the face has smaller spots.\nWait, some sources state: \"A giraffe's coat consists of brown patches separated by lighter hair.\"\nAnother source: \"How many spots does a giraffe have? Most giraffes have between 40 and 100 spots.\"\nIf it's 40-100, say 70 spots.\n110,000 * 70 = 7,700,000.\nBut wait, reticulated giraffes have many more spots. Reticulated giraffes can have 200+ spots.\nAverage of 40 and 300 is around 170. Let's say 150.\n110,000 * 150 = 16,500,000.\n\nLet's pick a reasonable